In this work, we aim to extend the success of universal text embedding models to the multimodal domain. We introduce the Massive Multimodal Embedding Benchmark (MMEB), which covers 4 meta-tasks (i.e. classification, visual question answering, multimodal retrieval, and visual grounding) and 36 datasets, including 20 training and 16 evaluation datasets. MMEB aims to provide a comprehensive evaluation of multimodal embedding models across different tasks and datasets. Furthermore, we propose VLM2Vec, a contrastive training framework that converts any state-of-the-art vision-language model into an embedding model via training on MMEB. Unlike previous models such as CLIP \cite{radford2021learning} and BLIP \cite{li2022blip}, VLM2Vec can process any combination of images and text to generate a fixed-dimensional vector based on task instructions. We build a series of VLM2Vec models on Phi-3.5-V \cite{abdin2024phi-3}, VLM2Vec achieves an absolute average improvement of 10\% to 20\% over existing multimodal embedding models on both in-distribution and out-of-distribution datasets in MMEB. <|section_end|>

